# The supervision decision, run by BOTH implementations of the rule:
#
#   lifecycle::supervise_action     (Rust, fleet-view/)  via fleet-view/src/lifecycle.rs
#   cerebro--supervise-action       (emacs/cerebro.el)   via emacs/cerebro-test.el
#
# WHY IT IS ONE FILE. It is `tests/lib/supervisor.cases`'s reason, one layer in: cb-kcs.5 is a
# cutover, so for a while both implementations answer about the same checkout, and a row they
# answer differently is a session ended twice or never. Read that file's header for the
# conventions this one copies deliberately.
#
# WHAT BOTH READERS MUST BIND. The rows assume `cerebro-end-grace' 30 and
# `cerebro-answer-timeout' 900, and `cerebro-interactive-answer-timeout' 1800, so a reader binds or
# passes exactly those numbers rather than whatever the running fleet is customised to. The two
# answer timeouts are per kind: an implementer's questions are ones the navigator answers, an
# interactive role's are ones they think about, so the second clock is twice the first.
#
# THE COLUMNS, tab- or space-separated, in order:
#
#   kind            `interactive' or `implementer' - the roster kind.
#   state           `working' `idle' `waiting' `asking' `dead' `up' `unknown' `standby' - the
#                   derived row state. `standby' arrived with cb-kcs.4.1, which gave the Rust
#                   `RowState' the variant and the row its blue dotted circle.
#   ours            `yes' when THIS view holds a live session for the agent. This is Emacs's
#                   `external' inverted (external = (not ours)): the two implementations answer
#                   the same question with opposite polarity, and the table speaks the positive
#                   one.
#
#                   THE ONE EXCEPTION IS `standby', and a reader that assumes the guard wraps
#                   everything will read those rows as a mistake. A standby row hosts no session,
#                   so `ours' is `no' on every one of them - and the first still answers `retire'.
#                   The two implementations agree because each guards on a different question:
#                   Rust's `ours' is "do I host a session I have not already stopped", and its
#                   standby arm sits AHEAD of that guard; Emacs's `external' is "up somewhere
#                   else", which a standby row is not, so its own guard lets the arm through. The
#                   question a standby row asks is neither - it is "is this name mine to disarm",
#                   and it is, by construction: the only way a row becomes `standby' is by being
#                   in this view's own armed set.
#   stop_flag       `yes' when `.cerebro/state/<name>.stop' exists.
#   idle_ends_pass  `yes' when this row's role is one whose `idle' means "my pass is over"
#                   (`cerebro-idle-ends-pass-roles', empty in this fleet).
#   stood           seconds since the state file's `since', or `none' for a missing or
#                   unparseable timestamp. `none' is NOT zero: a torn file must never read as an
#                   expired grace.
#   turn_ended_for  seconds since the state file's `turn_ended', or `none' for a row whose turn
#                   has not ended (or whose timestamp is missing). `none' is NOT zero, exactly as
#                   `stood''s `none' is not.
#   resume_stale    `yes' when this name was resumed while stuck and its state file has recorded
#                   nothing since. `no' otherwise, and `no' on every row that was never resumed.
#   action          `retire' `end' `nudge' `resume' or `none' - the answer.
#
# THE RULE THE ROWS ENCODE, stated once so a new row can be checked against intent:
#
#   * Not ours -> nothing at all, whatever the state and whatever the flag. A session this view
#     did not start is somebody else's to end, and a dead one stays dead.
#   * `idle', implementer -> retire under a flag, else nothing.
#   * `idle', interactive, role not in `idle_ends_pass' -> retire under a flag, else nothing.
#   * `idle', interactive, role in `idle_ends_pass' -> the end decision.
#   * `waiting', either kind -> the end decision. No kind guard: an implementer between beads has
#     ended its pass in exactly this sense (cb-1or.1).
#   * THE END DECISION: a flag retires at once and whatever the grace - nothing is in flight, so
#     there is nothing for a grace to protect. Otherwise `stood >= 30' ends it; otherwise nothing.
#   * `asking', implementer -> nudge once `stood >= 900'. The stop flag makes NO difference: the
#     bead is still in flight, so the question still needs an answer or a hand-back.
#   * `asking', interactive -> nudge once `stood >= 1800'. The stop flag makes no difference, for
#     the implementer arm's reason.
#   * `working', turn ended, stood past the ceiling -> the row is STUCK. Every other `working' row
#     answers nothing, as before.
#   * STUCK, not resumed yet -> `resume', both kinds. The stop flag makes NO difference, for the
#     `asking' arm's reason: the session is still holding work, so it still needs to move or hand
#     back.
#   * STUCK, and `resume_stale' -> the typed line did not take.
#       - implementer  -> nothing. It holds a claim, a worktree and possibly an open pull request,
#                         so nothing here ends it; `sweep-stalled' already offers `bd unclaim' for
#                         a bead a live session has not committed to for sixty minutes, and it
#                         skips only an `asking' session, so a stuck one is already offered there.
#       - interactive  -> the end decision WITHOUT its grace: a stop flag `retire's, otherwise
#                         `end'. It holds nothing, its buffer is kept as the record of the pass,
#                         and its own trigger starts a fresh session.
#   * `stood' is nothing to do with any of this. The grace it feeds is about a finished pass.
#   * everything else -> nothing, whatever the flag.
#
# Comparisons are `>=' on purpose: `>' delays every end by one tick.

# kind         state     ours stop idle_ends_pass stood  turn   resume action

# Not ours: the guard that wraps everything else.
implementer   waiting   no   yes  no   100    none   no   none
implementer   waiting   no   no   no   100    none   no   none
implementer   asking    no   no   no   1000   none   no   none
implementer   idle      no   yes  no   5      none   no   none
implementer   working   no   yes  no   100    none   no   none
interactive   waiting   no   yes  no   100    none   no   none
interactive   idle      no   yes  yes  100    none   no   none
interactive   asking    no   no   no   3000   none   no   none

# An implementer's idle: a flag retires it, nothing else acts.
implementer   idle      yes  yes  no   0      none   no   retire
implementer   idle      yes  no   no   5000   none   no   none
implementer   idle      yes  no   no   none   none   no   none

# An interactive role whose idle is just a live session with nothing in hand.
interactive   idle      yes  yes  no   0      none   no   retire
interactive   idle      yes  no   no   5000   none   no   none

# An interactive role whose idle IS the end of a pass: the end decision.
interactive   idle      yes  yes  yes  0      none   no   retire
interactive   idle      yes  no   yes  30     none   no   end
interactive   idle      yes  no   yes  31     none   no   end
interactive   idle      yes  no   yes  29     none   no   none
interactive   idle      yes  no   yes  none   none   no   none

# Waiting: the end decision for both kinds.
implementer   waiting   yes  yes  no   0      none   no   retire
implementer   waiting   yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   retire
implementer   waiting   yes  no   no   30     none   no   end
implementer   waiting   yes  no   no   31     none   no   end
implementer   waiting   yes  no   no   29     none   no   none
implementer   waiting   yes  no   no   0      none   no   none
implementer   waiting   yes  no   no   none   none   no   none
interactive   waiting   yes  yes  no   0      none   no   retire
interactive   waiting   yes  no   no   30     none   no   end
interactive   waiting   yes  no   no   29     none   no   none
interactive   waiting   yes  no   yes  30     none   no   end
interactive   waiting   yes  no   no   none   none   no   none

# Asking: both kinds, on their own clocks, and the flag makes no difference to either.
implementer   asking    yes  no   no   900    none   no   nudge
implementer   asking    yes  no   no   901    none   no   nudge
implementer   asking    yes  yes  no   900    none   no   nudge
implementer   asking    yes  no   no   899    none   no   none
implementer   asking    yes  no   no   none   none   no   none
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   1800   none   no   nudge
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   1801   none   no   nudge
interactive   asking    yes  yes  no   1800   none   no   nudge
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   1799   none   no   none
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   none   none   no   none
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   3000   none   no   nudge
interactive   asking    yes  yes  no   3000   none   no   nudge

# Every other state, whatever the flag.
implementer   working   yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none
implementer   dead      yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none
implementer   up        yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none
implementer   unknown   yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none
interactive   working   yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none
interactive   dead      yes  no   no   5000   none   no   none
interactive   up        yes  yes  no   5000   none   no   none

# Standby: no session hosted anywhere, so `ours' is `no' - and a flagged implementer is still
# disarmed rather than retried, because a stop flag written before its session died still says
# *no further bead* (cb-hzs). An interactive standby row's flag belongs to the start path.
implementer   standby   no   yes  no   none   none   no   retire
implementer   standby   no   no   no   none   none   no   none
interactive   standby   no   yes  no   none   none   no   none
interactive   standby   no   no   no   none   none   no   none

# A stuck row: `working', the turn has ended, and it has stood past the 30-minute ceiling.
# Both kinds are asked to carry on, once, and the stop flag makes no difference to that.
implementer   working   yes  no   no   5000   1800   no   resume
implementer   working   yes  no   no   5000   1801   no   resume
implementer   working   yes  yes  no   5000   1800   no   resume
implementer   working   yes  no   no   none   1800   no   resume
implementer   working   yes  no   no   0      1800   no   resume
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   1800   no   resume
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   9000   no   resume
interactive   working   yes  yes  no   5000   1800   no   resume
interactive   working   yes  no   yes  5000   1800   no   resume
interactive   working   yes  no   no   none   1800   no   resume

# Under the ceiling, or no turn-end at all: an ordinary working row.
implementer   working   yes  no   no   5000   1799   no   none
implementer   working   yes  no   no   5000   none   no   none
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   1799   no   none
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   none   no   none

# Not ours: the guard that wraps everything but `standby'.
implementer   working   no   no   no   5000   1800   no   none
interactive   working   no   yes  no   5000   1800   no   none

# The resume did not take. An implementer is never ended from here; an interactive role is.
implementer   working   yes  no   no   5000   1800   yes  none
implementer   working   yes  yes  no   5000   9000   yes  none
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   1800   yes  end
interactive   working   yes  no   no   5000   9000   yes  end
interactive   working   yes  no   no   none   1800   yes  end
interactive   working   yes  no   yes  5000   1800   yes  end
interactive   working   yes  yes  no   5000   1800   yes  retire
interactive   working   no   no   no   5000   1800   yes  none

# A row that is not `working' is never stuck, whatever its turn-end says.
interactive   asking    yes  no   no   100    9000   no   none
interactive   waiting   yes  no   no   0      9000   no   none
implementer   idle      yes  no   no   100    9000   no   none
implementer   standby   no   no   no   none   9000   no   none
interactive   dead      yes  no   no   5000   9000   yes  none
